  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">DailyBriefingCollector resolves event/matter/project candidate IDs through IMembershipResolverService; the bypass fan-out (ResolveOwnedIdsAsync at 236-239) and the "resolver bypass" log wording are removed — a grep of the collector shows no remaining bypass path for these three entities.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The test at DailyBriefingCollectorTests.cs:238 now asserts resolver routing (not bypass) and passes; if renamed, the new name reflects routing.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a systemUser who is a sprk_assignedattorney1 (collaborator) but NOT the owner of a matter, when the collector runs, then that matter is in the candidate set — the collaborator smoke test asserts this and fails against the old bypass.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given a systemUser who OWNS a matter/project/event, when the collector runs, then that record is still included — an owner-scoped regression test passes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">dotnet build src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/ exits 0 errors; the collector test project passes; publish size reported (absolute + diff vs 49.63 MB incl-PDBs); no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    See spec.md FR-C4, plan.md Phase B.4, notes/inbound-from-r7/03 item 1. This is the chain root for the collector chain (033→034→036→037) — all four touch DailyBriefingCollector.cs and must run sequentially. The pinned test re-flip is intentional (PR #558 forced this conversation). Gate G-R5-C is browser UAT (assigned attorney sees their matter) — the unit smoke test is necessary but not gate-sufficient.
  </notes>

